This repo is both an Obsidian vault and a Jekyll website for a tabletop RPG campaign.

The markdown files are the main source of truth.
The site is published with Jekyll, the Just the Docs theme, and GitHub Pages.

## First Steps

- Search existing notes before inventing new lore, NPCs, factions, places, mechanics, or names.
- Prefer local campaign files over memory.
- Treat `campaigns/`, `directory/`, `systems/`, and similar content folders as campaign source material.
- Treat `_site/` and `.jekyll-cache/` as generated output. Do not edit them directly.
- Preserve Obsidian-friendly markdown and Jekyll/Liquid syntax.

## Project Shape

Common folders:

- `campaigns/`: session logs, campaign arcs, chapter pages, play reports.
- `directory/`: gazetteer entries for places, NPCs, factions, organisations, and setting material.
- `systems/`: rules, procedures, character templates, subsystems, and generators.
- `imgs/`: images used by markdown pages and galleries.
- `_includes/`: reusable Jekyll snippets.
- `_sass/`: Just the Docs theme customisation.
- `_data/`: theme data files; engine link, gallery, and metadata extraction runs in-memory via the `oude-mechanismen` gem.
- `.obsidian/`: Obsidian vault settings. Avoid changing unless explicitly asked.

## Writing Style

- Use British English throughout.
- Prefer one sentence per line when writing conversational answers.
- Use short, readable, and clear sentences.
- Skip a line between paragraphs, but not with bulleted lists.
- For practical references, procedures, or exports, favour clarity over style.
- Do not over-explain in published notes.
- When uncertainty exists, mark it rather than invent continuity.
- No read-aloud blocks: never generate boxed narrative text (`> Table Read-Aloud`), scripted monologues, or descriptive paragraphs meant to be read to players.
- Notebook-centric delivery: the GM writes prep notes into a physical notebook. Provide the minimum word count necessary: 1-line sensory cues, 1-line tactical facts, and sharp, bulleted player choices.
- Pre-Flight Publishing Audit Gate (Pre-PR Only): To preserve LLM context and prevent unnecessary token consumption, the full 3-Gate Pre-Publishing Audit (Gate 1: `oude lint`; Gate 2: `review-style`; Gate 3: `oude lint-wiki`, surfaced by the `lint-wiki` skill) must NOT be executed on routine intermediate commits. It is strictly mandatory immediately prior to requesting final operator review or drafting a Pull Request (`gh pr create`), and whenever an operator asks "What is left before publishing?" or "Is this session ready to publish?". The agent MUST execute the 3-Gate Audit and emit the standardised Pre-Publishing Readiness Scorecard before declaring readiness. Declaring publication readiness on the basis of Git, CI, or Jekyll build status alone is strictly prohibited. Deterministic Gates 1 and 3 (`oude lint`, `oude lint-wiki`) execute locally with zero token consumption and may be run at any time.

## Campaign Canon & Canon Hierarchy

- **Tier 0 (raw session media, local disk only):** Raw session audio recordings and verbatim speech transcripts reside strictly on local disk at `~/Private/rpg-sessions/<campaign>/`, completely excluded from Git, Obsidian Sync, and all networked storage for player privacy.
- **Tier 1 canon** is the published campaign notes (listed in `.agents/context/tier1_sources.yml` and indexed locally in the gitignored `.agents/context/catalog.json` cache; notes may carry an optional explicit `id:` front matter used for Tier 2 joins). These notes are strong canon.
- **Tier 2 canon** is Project Memory, visible conversation, structured GM rulings and faction-turn records in `<drive_dir>/_gm-materials/rulings/` (indexed in the derived `<drive_dir>/_gm-materials/.cache/tier2.json`, compiled via `oude tier2`), and archival session notes in `<drive_dir>/_gm-materials/session-summaries/`.
- **Tier 3 canon** is reference material and structured digests in `<drive_dir>/_gm-materials/digests/` (indexed locally in the gitignored `.agents/context/references.json` cache) plus unplayed session prep in `<drive_dir>/_gm-materials/session-preps/`. Raw sourcebook PDFs stay outside the vault on local disk. It should be used as influence and possibility, not as automatic canon.

## Context & Token Optimisation Policy

- Never read all markdown notes or large reference PDFs in bulk.
- Use the `lookup-context` skill or consult the local `.agents/context/catalog.json` cache first to locate relevant entities, locations, and factions before reading files (if absent on a fresh clone, run `bundle exec jekyll build` or `oude doctor --fix`, or fall back to targeted `rg` searches).
- The catalogue is a derived navigation cache, not a replacement for reading the note or a version-controlled source file. Once located, open only the target file (using line-range reads where practical).
- For Tier 3 setting lore or procedures, consult `_gm-materials/digests/` via `.agents/context/references.json` before opening raw PDFs.
- Front-matter `hooks` field is deprecated and ignored across all agents and tooling. Do not add `hooks:` to new front matter.
- Regenerate the local Tier 1 catalogue cache with `bundle exec jekyll build` (or `oude doctor --fix`). Do not commit `.agents/context/catalog.json`, `.agents/context/rolling_glossary.md` (the derived rolling session glossary cache generated by `oude scribe`), `.agents/context/references.json` (the derived Tier 3 reference routing index generated by `oude digest`), or `_gm-materials/.cache/tier2.json` (the derived Tier 2 rulings index generated by `oude tier2`) to Git.
- For heavy batch text processing, extraction, or local summarisation, offload to local Ollama (`qwen2.5:14b`) over `localhost:11434`, always enforcing British English in prompt instructions.

## Markdown & Obsidian Conventions

- Preserve YAML front matter.
- Use existing front matter patterns such as `layout`, `title`, `parent`, `nav_order`, `type`, `image`, and `images`.
- Use relative markdown links for internal links, usually including the `.md` filename when linking source notes.
- Preserve Dataview queries, especially when they are inside Jekyll comments.
- Preserve Liquid includes such as `{% include header_directories.md %}` and gallery includes.
- Do not convert working Obsidian/Jekyll patterns into another linking style without being asked.
- Keep filenames and folder naming consistent with nearby files.

## Agent Behaviour

- **Branch Lifecycle**: Feature branches must be deleted immediately after their corresponding Pull Request is merged or closed. Never retain merged remote or local feature branches.
- **Post-Merge Cleanup**: When a Pull Request is merged or closed, switch to `main`, pull the latest remote changes (`git checkout main && git pull`), delete the local feature branch (`git branch -d <branch>`), and prune remote tracking references (`git fetch --prune`).

